A||||4||Turkey on Rye||Is Clutch Replacement Easy?||||1||
Z||000000||Turkey on Rye||11-13-2001||10:05 PM||||One of my friends burnt up my clutch and i need to replace it. Its a 4 speed on a 86 GT. Is it hard to do yourself and how is the install on it? An help would be awesome.||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||11-13-2001||10:25 PM||||No.  It sucks.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>It's doable if you have a means of lifting the car enough to get the engine/cradle assembly out from under it, and you are very patient.  Some people here claim to have done it without removing the cradle, but I don't see how.  The chilton's and haynes manuals both have usable procedures for this.<BR>I've done mine twice, and the easiest by far was dropping the entire engine/transaxle assembly as a unit, attached to the cradle, lifting the body up by the trunk lid catch with an engine hoist.  After blocking the body in the air, I slid the drivetrain assembly out and used the engine hoist to lift the engine off the transaxle.  All work is now easier to accomplish than if it was overhead on a hoist.  <BR>Also a good time to fix exhaust leaks and rear main seal, oil pan leaks, timing chain and gears, balancer seal leaks, poly bushing or spring swap, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ITB 2M4||11-13-2001||10:28 PM||||Having pulled the motor in my '84 three times, I can tell you it ain't easy.  The quickest way I've found to do it, is to pull the rear bumper cover off, along with the taillights, stick some heavy duty boards in the frame horns, and use an engine hoist with a strap going from one board, around the hoist, down to the other board, and lift the car off the engine cradle.  You'll have to take just about everything off the motor (a/c, alt., intake, exhaust) before you drop the motor out.  You can take the tranny out through the top, but it's gonna be tough aligning the input shaft in the clutch when it's time to put it back together.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000003||DR650SE||11-13-2001||10:59 PM||||Heres a link to do it by droping halfthe cradle.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/clutch.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/clutch.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>AIM:  DR650SE<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||12
Z||000004||Rodrv6||11-13-2001||11:02 PM||||I've used two methods that seem to work well depending on what equipment you have.<BR>The first method is to drop the whole cradle and engine assembly out the bottom and then separating the transmission from the engine. The advantage is it's very easy to work on once it's on the floor.  You need a dolly to support the cradle and a floor jack or hoist capable of lifting the back of the car high enough.  The disadvantage to this method is the need to drain the coolant and unhook fuel and electrical components.  It took about 3 hours to get the clutch out of my GT with this method.<BR>The other way is to support the engine with an overhead hoist and just remove the cradle. Then the transmission can be removed from underneath.  You don't have to mess with fluid and electrical stuff, but the exhaust pipe will have to be unbolted and the transmission is harder to install while trying to support it with a floorjack.  I did an 84 4 cylinder this way a couple of weeks ago and took about 6 hours to do the complete job.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
